# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `SetProperty` — value overload removed

EF Core 10 removed the `SetProperty(prop, TProperty value)` overload. The value argument **must** now be an `Expression<Func<TSource, TProperty>>` (a lambda), not a bare constant.

**Fix:** Wrap the constant in a lambda expression:

```csharp
var valueParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "v");
// Replace:  Expression.Constant("foo")
// With a lambda: v => "foo"
var valueLambda = Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
    Expression.Constant("foo"), valueParameter);

setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyCalls<Blog>, SetPropertyCalls<Blog>>>(
    Expression.Call(
        instance: setters.Body,
        methodName: nameof(SetPropertyCalls<Blog>.SetProperty),
        typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) },
        arguments: new Expression[]
        {
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
            valueLambda  // lambda instead of bare constant
        }),
    setters.Parameters);
```

---

## 2. Complex types — default column naming now prefixed

EF Core 10 prefixes complex-type property columns with the navigation name to avoid ambiguity. Your `ShippingAddress.Street` and `BillingAddress.Street` would both have mapped to `Street` before; now they default to `ShippingAddress_Street` / `BillingAddress_Street`. Existing databases will break.

**Fix:** Either rename DB columns via a migration, or explicitly preserve old names:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, a => {
        a.Property(x => x.Street).HasColumnName("Street");
        // ... other properties
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, a => {
        a.Property(x => x.Street).HasColumnName("BillingStreet");
    });
});

// For nested: Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b => {
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, p => {
        p.ComplexProperty(pay => pay.BillingAddress, a => {
            a.Property(x => x.City).HasColumnName("City");
        });
    });
});
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `CommandSource` parameter

EF Core 10 added a `CommandSource commandSource` parameter to the diagnostics interface methods.

**Fix:** Add the parameter:

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // ← new required parameter
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

---

**Summary:** The theme of EF Core 10 is removing ambiguous/convenient overloads in favor of explicit ones. Check the official [EF Core 10 breaking changes doc](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/what-is-new/ef-core-10.0/breaking-changes) for the full list — there may be additional interface members added beyond `CommandSource` depending on which methods you implement.
